--TEST--
A literal statement handed to `stream` is validated while checking, exactly as `query`'s is: the placeholders are counted against the literal parameter array before the program is ever run — `rule:core-classes/db-statement-members` and `rule:core-classes/db-compile-time-query-checking`, `rule:expressions/intrinsic-list-is-closed`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:core-classes/db-compile-time-query-checking` validates a *literal* SQL argument during checking — the
// placeholder count against a literal params array — and § 4 gives `stream` the
// same statement and the same binding rule `query` has. So the check follows the
// rule rather than the member: a mistake that `query` finds at compile time is
// not one `stream` finds at the first row.
//
// This is the boundary worth pinning, because a member reaches that pass only by
// being on `nvs_types::intrinsics`' closed list. A `stream` left off it would
// compile this file, run it, and report the same mistake from the wire — later,
// on a connection it had already made busy.
var $db = Core\Db::connect("main");

var $walk = $db->stream("select id from accounts where owner = ? and status = ?", ["ada"]);

// And the same question asked of the other class that declares the row. ADR
// 0043's delegation gives `Core\Db\Transaction` the member under the
// connection's symbol, and § 10's list is nominal against the *declaring*
// class — so a row present on one and absent on the other would be a statement
// checked or not depending on which receiver the call site happened to hold.
$db->transaction(fn (Core\Db\Transaction $tx): void => {
    $tx->stream("select id from accounts where owner = ? and status = ?", ["ada"]);
});
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0770]: this query and its parameters do not agree: the statement has 2 `?` placeholder(s) and was given 1 argument(s), and `rule:core-classes/db-parameters` binds them one for one — an `inList` counts as the one argument it is, however many values it holds
   --> case.nvs:14:25
%A
error: aborting due to 2 errors
